        "# GSM Security — Interactive Teaching Laboratory\n",
        "\n",
        "**Course:** Security of Mobile Systems  \n",
        "**Format:** Guided Jupyter practical with instructor discussion and student experiments  \n",
        "**Suggested duration:** 75–90 minutes (adjust to class pace)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning outcomes\n",
        "By the end, students should be able to (1) explain GSM subscriber authentication with Ki, RAND, and SRES; (2) distinguish subscriber authentication from network authentication; (3) describe how TMSI affects linkability; (4) explain the purpose of the A8-derived session key Kc; (5) illustrate XOR-based stream encryption; and (6) identify the assumptions behind replay, key-compromise, and keystream-reuse demonstrations.\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Scope and safety:** All subscribers, messages, keys, and network entities are fictional. The notebook runs locally, does not contact GSM infrastructure, and does not capture real traffic. The functions `demo_A3`, `demo_A8`, and `demo_A5` use HMAC-SHA256 as **educational substitutes**; they are **not** COMP128, A5/1, or other deployed GSM algorithms. Real GSM security has additional protocol details.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dependencies:** Python 3, `matplotlib`, `numpy` (install with `%pip install matplotlib numpy` if needed). Run cells from top to bottom. Fresh random challenges mean hexadecimal outputs change each run.\n",
        "\n",
        "### GSM security story\n",
        "**SIM and AuC share Ki → network sends RAND → SIM returns SRES (A3) → SIM and network derive Kc (A8) → mobile and base-station-side equipment use Kc for radio-link encryption (A5).** TMSI helps avoid repeatedly revealing the permanent IMSI, but does not guarantee anonymity."
      ]
