        "## 1. Subscriber authentication — legitimate network\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Part 1 — GSM Subscriber Authentication (A3)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Scenario 1: Legitimate Subscriber Authentication\n",
        "\n",
        "**Objective:** Understand how a GSM network authenticates a subscriber using a challenge-response mechanism.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background\n",
        "\n",
        "In GSM, the SIM card and the mobile operator share a secret key called **Ki**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The authentication process works as follows:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. The network generates a random 128-bit challenge (**RAND**).\n",
        "2. The network sends RAND to the mobile station.\n",
        "3. The SIM uses the secret key Ki and algorithm A3 to calculate a 32-bit response (**SRES**).\n",
        "4. The network independently calculates the expected SRES using the same Ki and RAND.\n",
        "5. If both responses match, the subscriber is authenticated.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** The secret key Ki is never transmitted over the radio interface.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### GSM Security Limitation\n",
        "\n",
        "GSM provides **one-way authentication**: the network authenticates the subscriber, but the SIM does not authenticate the network.\n",
        "\n",
        "This limitation is relevant to fake base-station attacks.\n",
        "\n",
        "### About this demonstration\n",
        "\n",
        "We use Python's HMAC-SHA256 as an educational substitute for A3. This is **not the real GSM A3/COMP128 algorithm**, but it illustrates the challenge-response principle.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Before running the code\n",
        "\n",
        "Predict: What will happen if the SIM and the network use different secret keys?\n"
      ]
    },
