        "\n",
        "## Part 2 — Replay Attack: Why Fresh RAND Matters\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this experiment, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Explain how a replay attack works.\n",
        "- Understand why GSM uses a random challenge (RAND).\n",
        "- Distinguish a fresh challenge from a reused challenge.\n",
        "- Explain why knowing an old SRES does not normally allow\n",
        "  an attacker to authenticate with a new RAND.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Scenario: An Attacker Records an Authentication Response\n",
        "\n",
        "In GSM, the SIM and the operator share a secret key, Ki.\n",
        "\n",
        "The network sends a 128-bit random challenge (RAND), and\n",
        "the SIM calculates a 32-bit response (SRES).\n",
        "\n",
        "An attacker can observe the authentication exchange and\n",
        "record the transmitted SRES, but does not know Ki.\n",
        "\n",
        "We investigate four cases:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Case | Description | Expected result |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| 1 | Legitimate SIM responds to RAND1 | Accepted |\n",
        "| 2 | Attacker replays SRES1 for fresh RAND2 | Rejected |\n",
        "| 3 | Legitimate SIM responds to RAND2 | Accepted |\n",
        "| 4 | Network mistakenly reuses RAND1 | Old SRES1 accepted |\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important Security Concept: Freshness\n",
        "\n",
        "A fresh random challenge makes a previously recorded\n",
        "authentication response normally unusable.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, if the network reuses a previous RAND, the\n",
        "corresponding old SRES may become valid again.\n",
        "\n",
        "The fourth case deliberately simulates an insecure network\n",
        "implementation. It is not the expected behavior of a\n",
        "properly operating GSM authentication system.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Connection to Cryptography\n",
        "\n",
        "We continue using our educational A3 function:\n",
        "\n",
        "    SRES = HMAC-SHA256(Ki, RAND)[:4]\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not the historical GSM COMP128 implementation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Before Running\n",
        "\n",
        "Predict:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Can an attacker calculate SRES2 without knowing Ki?\n",
        "2. Why should RAND change between authentication attempts?\n",
        "3. What happens if the network repeats RAND1?\n"
      ]
