        "\n",
        "## Part 3 — Fake Base Station: Missing Network Authentication\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "After completing this experiment, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Explain the difference between subscriber authentication\n",
        "   and network authentication.\n",
        "2. Identify why original GSM is vulnerable to network impersonation.\n",
        "3. Explain why knowing Ki is not necessary to broadcast a\n",
        "   claimed network identity.\n",
        "4. Distinguish a conceptual security weakness from a real\n",
        "   radio-based attack.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background: One-Way Authentication\n",
        "\n",
        "In original GSM, authentication is asymmetric.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Subscriber authentication:**\n",
        "- The network challenges the SIM using RAND.\n",
        "- The SIM calculates SRES using Ki.\n",
        "- The network verifies SRES.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Network authentication:**\n",
        "- The SIM does not receive a cryptographic proof of\n",
        "  the GSM network's identity.\n",
        "- Consequently, the SIM cannot verify a base station's\n",
        "  legitimacy through mutual authentication.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Experiment\n",
        "\n",
        "We compare three conceptual situations:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Scenario | Network claim | Cryptographic verification |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| 1 | Legitimate GSM network | No network-authentication check |\n",
        "| 2 | Fake base station | No network-authentication check |\n",
        "| 3 | Hypothetical mutually authenticated network | Invalid proof rejected |\n",
        "\n",
        "The third scenario illustrates how a cryptographic\n",
        "network-authentication check could change the outcome.\n",
        "It is NOT a feature of original GSM.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "This experiment does not transmit radio signals, create a\n",
        "real base station, force a handset to connect, or extract Ki.\n",
        "\n",
        "It demonstrates only the missing network-authentication\n",
        "security property.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why is authenticating the SIM insufficient?\n",
        "2. Can a claimed network identity prove legitimacy?\n",
        "3. Does a fake base station automatically know Ki?\n",
        "4. What additional security property is needed to\n",
        "   authenticate both parties?\n"
      ]
    },
