        "\n",
        "## Part 6 — Backhaul Eavesdropping:\n",
        "## Why Radio Encryption Is Not End-to-End Encryption\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "After this experiment, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Distinguish radio-link encryption from end-to-end encryption.\n",
        "- Explain where GSM air-interface encryption terminates.\n",
        "- Identify the security risk of an unencrypted backhaul link.\n",
        "- Explain how additional backhaul encryption reduces exposure.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background\n",
        "\n",
        "In GSM, air-interface encryption protects communication\n",
        "between the Mobile Station (MS) and the Base Transceiver\n",
        "Station (BTS).\n",
        "\n",
        "However, protecting the radio interface does not\n",
        "automatically protect the entire communication path.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the following simplified architecture:\n",
        "\n",
        "Mobile Station → BTS → Microwave Backhaul → MSC\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume that:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. The mobile-to-BTS radio link is encrypted.\n",
        "2. The BTS decrypts the radio traffic.\n",
        "3. The BTS forwards the recovered message over an\n",
        "   unencrypted backhaul link.\n",
        "4. An attacker can observe that backhaul link.\n",
        "\n",
        "Under these assumptions, the attacker can read the message\n",
        "even though the original radio transmission was encrypted.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Experiment\n",
        "\n",
        "We compare three situations:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Scenario | Communication link | Attacker observes |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| A | Encrypted mobile-to-BTS link | Ciphertext |\n",
        "| B | Unencrypted BTS-to-MSC backhaul | Plaintext |\n",
        "| C | Encrypted BTS-to-MSC backhaul | Ciphertext |\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important Technical Notes\n",
        "\n",
        "- This is a simplified demonstration, not an implementation\n",
        "  of GSM A5/1 or A5/3.\n",
        "- We use a modern authenticated encryption algorithm to\n",
        "  illustrate encryption and decryption.\n",
        "- In a real GSM architecture, BTS, BSC, and MSC functions\n",
        "  and transport links may be separated.\n",
        "- Not all GSM microwave backhaul links are unencrypted.\n",
        "- This simulation does not intercept actual network traffic.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Questions for Students\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why can the backhaul attacker read the message in Scenario B?\n",
        "2. Does encryption between the mobile and BTS guarantee\n",
        "   end-to-end confidentiality?\n",
        "3. What changes when backhaul encryption is enabled?\n",
        "4. Would encrypting the message end-to-end protect it even\n",
        "   if an intermediate transport link were unencrypted?\n"
      ]
    },
