        "# part 7: GSM Security Lab — TMSI Identification, Authentication, and Reallocation\n",
        "\n",
        "**Based on:** *Pseudonymization over the Air Interface (1)*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Learning objectives:** Understand the difference between identification and authentication, how GSM protects subscriber identities, and how a new TMSI is assigned.\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Note:** This notebook simulates the unknown-old-TMSI scenario shown in the slide. HMAC-SHA256 and AES-CTR are educational substitutes, not actual GSM A3/A8/A5 implementations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. GSM Identification and Authentication Process\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 1 — Mobile Sends Old TMSI and LAI\n",
        "\n",
        "The mobile station (MS) sends its previously assigned TMSI and Location Area Identity (LAI).\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "MS → Network\n",
        "Old TMSI = A1B2C3D4\n",
        "LAI = 262-01-1001\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The TMSI helps avoid repeatedly exposing the permanent IMSI.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 2 — VLR Attempts Identification\n",
        "\n",
        "The Visitor Location Register (VLR) attempts to find the IMSI associated with the old TMSI.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Mapping succeeds:** The network identifies the subscriber and can proceed with authentication.\n",
        "- **Mapping fails:** The network may request the permanent IMSI.\n",
        "\n",
        "In this slide, **the mapping fails**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 3 — Identity Request and Response\n",
        "\n",
        "The network requests the subscriber's IMSI.\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Network → MS: Identity Request\n",
        "MS → Network: Identity Response (IMSI)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "**Privacy risk:** If ciphering is not active, the IMSI can be exposed over the radio interface.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 4 — Subscriber Authentication (A3)\n",
        "\n",
        "The network sends a random challenge, `RAND`, to the SIM.\n",
        "\n",
        "The SIM uses its secret key `Ki` to calculate:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "SRES=A3(K_i,RAND)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "The network compares the received SRES with the expected response.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Match → Authentication succeeds.\n",
        "- Mismatch → Authentication fails.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** TMSI mapping identifies the subscriber; A3 verifies possession of the subscriber's secret key.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 5 — Session Key Derivation (A8)\n",
        "\n",
        "The SIM and home network derive the ciphering key:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "K_c=A8(K_i,RAND)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "`Kc` is a 64-bit key used by GSM A5 ciphering.\n",
        "\n",
        "**A8 generates Kc; A5 uses Kc.**\n",
        "\n",
        "## 2. TMSI Reallocation\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 6 — Network Generates New TMSI\n",
        "\n",
        "After successful identification and authentication, the VLR prepares a new temporary identity.\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Old TMSI = A1B2C3D4\n",
        "New TMSI = 7F92E0AB\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The network retains the necessary mapping information until reassignment is confirmed.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 7 — Activate A5 Ciphering\n",
        "\n",
        "GSM A5 uses `Kc` and radio frame information to protect radio communication.\n",
        "\n",
        "Conceptually, for a stream-cipher variant:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "C=P\\oplus Keystream\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "In our Python simulation:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **AES-CTR:** Provides confidentiality.\n",
        "- **HMAC-SHA256:** Provides message integrity and authentication.\n",
        "\n",
        "These are educational adaptations; classic GSM A5 does not provide cryptographic message integrity.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 8 — TMSI Reallocation Command\n",
        "\n",
        "The network sends the new TMSI over the ciphered radio connection.\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Network → MS\n",
        "TMSI Reallocation Command\n",
        "New TMSI = 7F92E0AB\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The mobile decrypts the message and stores the new TMSI.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 9 — Reallocation Complete\n",
        "\n",
        "The mobile acknowledges successful reallocation.\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "MS → Network\n",
        "TMSI Reallocation Complete\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The VLR can then remove the old TMSI mapping.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3. Summary of GSM Security Mechanisms\n",
        "\n",
        "| Mechanism | Purpose |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| IMSI | Permanent subscriber identification |\n",
        "| TMSI | Temporary identity for privacy |\n",
        "| VLR mapping | Resolve TMSI to IMSI |\n",
        "| A3 | Subscriber authentication |\n",
        "| A8 | Derive session ciphering key `Kc` |\n",
        "| A5 | Radio confidentiality |\n",
        "| TMSI reallocation | Reduce subscriber tracking |\n",
        "| AES-CTR (simulation) | Encryption |\n",
        "| HMAC-SHA256 (simulation) | Integrity and message authentication |\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4. Questions for Students\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why does the VLR perform TMSI-to-IMSI mapping before authentication?\n",
        "2. What happens if the old TMSI cannot be recognized?\n",
        "3. Why is successful mapping not sufficient for authentication?\n",
        "4. What is the difference between A3, A8, and A5?\n",
        "5. Why should the new TMSI be transmitted under ciphering?\n",
        "6. What security property does HMAC-SHA256 add to our simulation?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Key Takeaway\n",
        "\n",
        "**Identification → Authentication → Key Derivation → Ciphering → TMSI Reallocation**\n",
        "\n",
        "GSM uses TMSI to reduce exposure of the permanent IMSI, A3 to authenticate the subscriber, A8 to derive the ciphering key, and A5 to protect radio communication. TMSI reassignment further reduces the ability to track subscribers through a persistent temporary identifier.\n"
      ]
    },
