        "\n",
        "## Part 8 — TMSI Privacy: Can a Subscriber Be Tracked?\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "After this experiment, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Explain the difference between IMSI and TMSI.\n",
        "- Understand why GSM uses temporary subscriber identifiers.\n",
        "- Identify how repeated identifiers enable activity linking.\n",
        "- Explain why changing TMSI improves privacy but does not\n",
        "  guarantee anonymity.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background\n",
        "\n",
        "**IMSI (International Mobile Subscriber Identity)**\n",
        "\n",
        "- A permanent subscriber identifier stored in the SIM.\n",
        "- Used by the mobile network to identify the subscriber.\n",
        "- Repeated exposure can make subscriber activity easier to link.\n",
        "\n",
        "**TMSI (Temporary Mobile Subscriber Identity)**\n",
        "\n",
        "- A temporary identifier assigned by the serving network.\n",
        "- Helps avoid transmitting IMSI unnecessarily.\n",
        "- Can be reassigned to reduce direct identifier linkability.\n",
        "- Is meaningful within the relevant serving-network context.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Experiment\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine a subscriber performing five activities:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Sending a message\n",
        "2. Making a call\n",
        "3. Accessing a network service\n",
        "4. Making another call\n",
        "5. Sending another message\n",
        "\n",
        "We compare three hypothetical scenarios:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Scenario | Identifier behavior | Privacy consequence |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| A | Same TMSI across all activities | Activities directly linkable |\n",
        "| B | Different TMSI for every activity | Direct identifier matching prevented |\n",
        "| C | Same IMSI exposed repeatedly | Activities linkable using a permanent identity |\n",
        "\n",
        "Scenario B is an idealized example of frequent reassignment,\n",
        "not a claim that GSM changes TMSI after every activity.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important Security Concept: Linkability\n",
        "\n",
        "**Linkability** means determining whether two or more\n",
        "observations belong to the same subscriber.\n",
        "\n",
        "A repeated TMSI allows an observer to associate activities\n",
        "without necessarily knowing the subscriber's real identity.\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing TMSI reduces direct identifier matching, but\n",
        "tracking may still be possible through timing, location,\n",
        "radio characteristics, or other information.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why is transmitting IMSI repeatedly a privacy risk?\n",
        "2. Does a repeated TMSI reveal the subscriber's real name?\n",
        "3. Can activities be linked even when the TMSI changes?\n",
