        "\n",
        "## Part 9 — Inside GSM A5/1: Registers and Majority Clocking\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "After this experiment, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Identify the three A5/1 shift registers.\n",
        "- Explain how Linear Feedback Shift Registers (LFSRs) work.\n",
        "- Understand majority clocking.\n",
        "- Explain why only two or three registers advance per step.\n",
        "- Distinguish historical A5/1 from our earlier HMAC-based simulation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Background\n",
        "\n",
        "A5/1 is a historical GSM stream cipher that uses three\n",
        "Linear Feedback Shift Registers (LFSRs).\n",
        "\n",
        "| Register | Length | Clock-Control Bit |\n",
        "|----------|--------|-------------------|\n",
        "| R1       | 19 bits | 8                 |\n",
        "| R2       | 22 bits | 10                |\n",
        "| R3       | 23 bits | 10                |\n",
        "\n",
        "Each register stores a sequence of bits.\n",
        "\n",
        "During clocking, feedback bits are calculated using XOR,\n",
        "and the selected registers shift by one position.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Majority Clocking\n",
        "\n",
        "A5/1 does not always advance all three registers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Read the clock-control bit from each register.\n",
        "2. Calculate the majority value (0 or 1).\n",
        "3. Advance registers whose clock-control bit matches\n",
        "   the majority.\n",
        "4. Leave the remaining register unchanged.\n",
        "\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "R1 clock bit = 1\n",
        "R2 clock bit = 0\n",
        "R3 clock bit = 1\n",
        "\n",
        "Majority = 1\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore:\n",
        "\n",
        "- R1 advances.\n",
        "- R2 remains unchanged.\n",
        "- R3 advances.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Keystream Generation\n",
        "\n",
        "After initialization with Kc and the GSM frame number,\n",
        "A5/1 performs 100 majority-clocked warm-up cycles.\n",
        "\n",
        "It then produces 228 keystream bits:\n",
        "\n",
        "- 114 bits for one transmission direction.\n",
        "- 114 bits for the other transmission direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "At each output step, the three register output bits\n",
        "are combined using XOR.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important Note\n",
        "\n",
        "This experiment implements the historical A5/1 design\n",
        "for educational purposes.\n",
        "\n",
        "A5/1 is a legacy cipher with known cryptographic weaknesses.\n",
        "It should not be considered secure for modern applications.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Discussion Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why does A5/1 use three registers?\n",
        "2. What is the purpose of majority clocking?\n",
        "3. Why do at least two registers advance at each step?\n",
        "4. What is the difference between A5/1 and our HMAC demo?\n"
      ]
    },
